{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966657-how-to-manage-rules-and-content","id":966657,"title":"How to manage rules and content?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-09-10T00:02:13-07:00","id":966657,"title":"How to manage rules and content?","content":"As far as Chronicles of Darkness games go, I have simply ran a small 10 session chronicle of Werewolves, managing a complete story. I had trouble in that game managing the different moving parts, even though I had straight forward ideas about what was supposed to happen. <br /> <br /> Mage is overwhelming in terms of its rules and how those rules interact with its setting. I open to the possibility that this is just me being unfamiliar with the material, but it does seem more complex than most RPGs in terms of both its setting and mechanics. I would like to open a discussion on how to run the game in general terms. <br /> <br /> I will start with two primary questions, but I am open to discussing any comments on any ideas about running this particular game. <br /> <br /> 1) How do you build a setting which has new mechanics for many different &quot;locations&quot;. To be specific, if read the development article Terra Ingcognita, <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/terra-incognita-mage-the-awakening/\" target=\"_blank\">http://theonyxpath.com/terra-incogni...the-awakening/</a>, you will see the variety of mystical locations in addition to physical geography present in a game world. Many of these places have their own specific rules to remember about them, so generating content for them isn't the same as generating a lot of content for a familiar human city. Not only are the mechanics different, but each type of location should feel different and play a different role in a story. So the Shadow and the Astral Plane are not just mechanically different, but I as a storyteller should provide a different mood for each place in those realms. <br /> <br /> <br /> 2) How do you manage all of the different mechanics when actually running a session? As an example, do you keep track of Nimbus effects consistently, or do you only bring it up when you want to and forget about its existence most of the time? If you are running a new game with new players, what would be something you can focus on at first, and what elements could you introduce gradually later?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8619-aforgeforthoughts","name":"AForgeForThoughts","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-10T00:15:56-07:00","id":966662,"title":"","content":"I should contribute as well. This is what I have so far, to narrow down my expectations of what a Mage session should be like. This should serve as an example of what kind of ideas I am trying to develop. <br /> <br /> <div align=\"center\"><b>Story Structure</b></div> <br /> <b>Fight Against Oppression:</b> Generic opposition to surveillance, nationalism, war, theocracy. Play up the cold and terrifying aspects of oppression. This aspect of a story involves <b>blame assignment </b>and <b>moral judgement.</b><br /> <br /> <b>Hermetic Mysteries:</b> Discovery of magic implemented through mechanical achievement or roleplaying is important. Players should create their own rituals for fun, and gain from this. At these parts of the story, the player deepens her understanding of magic and the fallen world. This part of the story is about <b>Awe </b>and <b>Inspiration</b>.<br /> <br /> <b>Tale of Hubris:</b> A mage filled without moral condemnation and filled with the confidence in their occult knowledge are extremely prone to monstrous forms of hubris. They will probably end up acting worse than Seers of the Throne, and this is the part of the story about <b>Skepticism </b>and <b>Fallibility. </b><br /> <br /> <b>NPCs: </b>For the most part, they can be accessed through social networks and phones. Most NPC interaction is masturbatory. <br /> <br /> <b>Others:</b> Ephemeral beings like Spirits, Ghosts, Goetia, and Angels will be common. Full template supernaturals will be rarer. Geists, Demons, and Mummies for example won\u2019t be out there unless one of the player characters is really interested in seeing those things. Most of these things want to stay hidden and not have a crazy wizard investigating their nature.<br /> <div align=\"center\"><b>Tropes, Themes, and Tech</b><br /> </div> <b>High Concept Imagery:</b> Mage is a game about the meaningful, so the mundane should be used for jarring effect. High mindedness and perceived profundity are important to the sense of being a mage. Big picture ideas about the world, and symbolic featuring of them should enter into the scene quite often. Try not to come off as pretentious. <br /> <br /> <b>Aesthetic Puzzles:</b> These kinds of puzzles are based off of aesthetics and metaphor. The way the players overcome them is through storytelling and interpretation. It is more important that the answer to any given question come from the Mage rather than the player, and that is what satisfies the puzzle. <br /> <br /> Example: On the statue of the cyclops reads \u201cFor I was blinded by the lie, but was given less by the truth. To enter my domain for knowledge what will be replaced for less?\u201d This apparently non-sense inscription on an Atlantean ruin could warrant many different responses from different mages. <br /> <br /> <b>Varied Procedure: </b>Mage offers a lot of procedures for resolving specific kinds of conflicts. Chases, Combats, Investigations, Social Maneuvering and their new Mage extensions are great ways to build interactive scenes. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8619-aforgeforthoughts","name":"AForgeForThoughts","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-10T00:39:26-07:00","id":966668,"title":"","content":"Index cards.<br /> <br /> Might sound kind of simplistic, but it's remarkably simple way to organize Conditions (or just get the official Condition cards), Tilts, and special rules for different environments/realms/etc.<br /> <br /> Also, one of the big things that 2e encourages by having a lot of things be Conditions (that then generate Beats if resolved) is to incentivize the players to be more proactive in making sure the rules are managed. There's a lot in 2e that has built in game rewards for players that take some of the burden off the ST.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-10T06:25:04-07:00","id":966727,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AForgeForThoughts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966657-how-to-manage-rules-and-content#post966657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2) How do you manage all of the different mechanics when actually running a session? As an example, do you keep track of Nimbus effects consistently, or do you only bring it up when you want to and forget about its existence most of the time? If you are running a new game with new players, what would be something you can focus on at first, and what elements could you introduce gradually later?</div> </div> </div> </div>I never had problem managing all the mechanics.....however i have player get overwhelmed by them as starting Pcs have to many stuff to choose and they dont know the meaning or significance of them.<br /> <br /> Example: they had to choose order, path, rotes, merits, praxis, tools, nimbus, arcanums and after all of that they them have to remember their arcanums scope. And in my experience for player unexperience with mage is overwhelming as they would forget/ignore half of those mechanics.<br /> <br /> So my solution for my campaign was for them to start as newly awakened (maximun 2 in an arcanum), no order, 1 tool (if any). No rotes, no orders, no nimbus, no praxies. And they learn at the &quot;same speed&quot; as their PCs what everything means. I did the same with WtA which is the second crunchiest game in WoD and by the end of the campaign the player themselves knew the mechanics as well as me.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-10T07:37:05-07:00","id":966748,"title":"","content":"Have you ever watched the Mcbane story on the Simpsons? It's a complete story that appears over multiple episodes for a few seconds at a time. Maybe you've watched Dr. Who instead and have noticed that while having completely random adventures there's always some little tie in to the season finale even if the Dr and his companions don't see it. That's how I run the Nimbus when it isn't being directly manipulated by the characters. I'll draw on the character for inspiration and craft an underlying loose story structure that I can pop into the main story for a moment whenever I want. Having the nimbus have its own story structure like that keeps me from forgetting it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-10T10:37:16-07:00","id":966822,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966657-how-to-manage-rules-and-content?p=966727#post966727\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So my solution for my campaign was for them to start as newly awakened (maximun 2 in an arcanum), no order, 1 tool (if any). No rotes, no orders, no nimbus, no praxies. And they learn at the &quot;same speed&quot; as their PCs what everything means. I did the same with WtA which is the second crunchiest game in WoD and by the end of the campaign the player themselves knew the mechanics as well as me.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is a good suggestion. I think they will have a better understanding of what the mechanics do when it was something they chose to improve upon their limited characters first. Scaling it back to be about a cabal of new mages may make setting development easier as well because the players and their characters won't entirely understand what is available. <br /> <br /> @Mrmdubois<br /> <br /> So you are tying the more subtle mechanics and story elements to the climax of the story? Do you do this with mechanics other than Nimbus?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8619-aforgeforthoughts","name":"AForgeForThoughts","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-10T11:09:33-07:00","id":966835,"title":"","content":"Not quite, it's more like I'm running several stories contiguously, one is the main story and then other stories sneak in cameos. It's just a convenient framework for remembering all the things that are affecting a character, order, nimbus, legacy, Aspirations, Obsessions, etc and it's a really back end kind of thing, I doubt the player will necessarily pick up on it unless they're really paying attention, much like you could watch a ton of the Simpsons and never notice that there's an entire other story playing out inside the series. Since I've laid out all those bits as their own plots though, I can remember the various mechanics and impactful stuff on characters that exists because stories are inherently easier to remember than dice rules. Being back end it's also easier to edit in case the characters change things up in a way I wasn't expecting, because if it doesn't happen right in front of the character's face then it never happened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-10T19:34:06-07:00","id":967081,"title":"","content":"I definitely forget about Nimbus effects 99% of the time unless I specifically want to bring it up, so that's one answer. If one of my players had a Nimbus-related Merit and wanted it to be important, I'd probably be more cognizant of it.<br /> <br /> Index cards are a very, very good idea. Just a quick summary of &quot;HOW PARADOX WORKS,&quot; &quot;WHAT'S EVERYONE'S NIMBUS,&quot; &quot;WHAT'S EVERYONE'S ASPIRATIONS/OBSESSION/VIRTUE AND VICE&quot; is extremely helpful to be able to glance at in play.<br /> <br /> Describing things like the Shadow, the Astral, ley lines and hallows and nodes, that's a bit difficult. The Astral is a little easier because it's actually divided into discrete areas: the PCs are always going to be in one Temenos realm at a time with its own overarching theme that ties everything together, or one specific memory or idea in the Oneiros, or whatever.<br /> <br /> As for other places...my usual approach to game prep is &quot;write down a bunch of short little idea fragments so I have a grab bag of stuff I can sprinkle into a session as it becomes relevant.&quot; Try this: write down like 10 different resonances that any given ley line might have, and if your players ask about a particular line, you'll have an answer ready that you can build on. You can try something similar with the Shadow. Write down ~10 types of spirits you think would be interesting and that your group might encounter in your setting, and the next time they peer across the Gauntlet, pick one of them and build the scene around it.<br /> <br /> You don't need to map everything out in advance, you just need an idea of what would be interesting that's general enough to slot into a scene.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>2E Legacy Updates</b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/978498-2e-legacy-update-brotherhood-of-the-demon-wind\" target=\"_blank\">Brotherhood of the Demon Wind</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/979249-2e-legacy-update-choir-of-hashmallim-with-bonus-content-about-summoning\" target=\"_blank\">Choir of Hashmallim</a> (plus extra Summoning content)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/980550-2e-legacy-update-storm-keepers\" target=\"_blank\">Storm Keepers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2207-caladriu","name":"Caladriu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1048"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T05:00:59-07:00","id":968456,"title":"","content":"How I do it: one step at a time. Mage is a complex game, so you should be ready to fall back to the basics if the rules start overwhelming you. Basic rolls, Down &amp; Dirty Magic, Mage Sight, Wisdom rules, Willpower and beats acquisition. Avoid combat scenes at first, focus on the strengths of the game, which is solving mysteries. Have index cards for Wisdom Conditions, perhaps one or two more like Inspired and Informed. Print out the spellcasting charts, make a list of the players' Paths, Aspirations, Obsessions and Nimbi. Think about potential descriptions of the Supernal World your player characters might perceive. Be ready to forget stuff, to misinterpret rules and adjudicate on the fly. Make sure your players know you're still learning and ask them for help, but only if it does not bog down the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-14T01:45:00-07:00","id":969092,"title":"","content":"First off, I feel your pain <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8619-aforgeforthoughts\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"8619\" data-vbnamecard=\"8619\">AForgeForThoughts</a>. MAGE comes with a hand basket of rules that can be intimidating. My players and I are 13 sessions into our chronicle, and we're still scratching our heads. (We mixed up the mechanics for both Rote Skills and Rote Spells) Make a 'cheat sheet' - index cards, printed pages, and whatnot. For combat turns, I plan to use poker chips with the initials of the PCs (plus reusable ones for opponents). Consider glass beads or other tokens to represent Mana, Reach, and Willpower.<br /> <br /> We also have a list of everyone's Mage Sight phenomenon. (The Obrimos player sees golden sigils, the Acanthus player sees ribbons, chains, and strings of fate, etc.)<br /> <br /> For locations, it might help if you decide first on what you want your players to explore, then apply rules that make sense. The PCs discover a haunted train station with phantasmal passengers who come and go. How might that operate in game terms? Is it an Avernian Gate? Is Time magic involved? Working your way backwards can provide clarity.<br /> <br /> Lastly, don't beat yourself too hard. If I were to choose between building a compelling narrative or muddling through all the rules just to be 'by the book', I would go for narrative. The rules kick in when the players need to see if their efforts succeed or fail. Good luck!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4260-terpsichordean","name":"terpsichordean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4260_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"79"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-14T02:33:45-07:00","id":969105,"title":"","content":"<br /> <br /> I think that I will want to place reminders in my notes to at least demonstrate a mechanic a few times so the players can see what it is for, and later on initiate themselves. Basically, I can write my notes for a scene I have planned to focus on one or two mechanics for that scene that I want to be interesting. <br /> <br /> The idea is though I could have a classic five room dungeon. Something with an entrance. a story room, a red hearing or set back, a climactic room, and a room with a twist or reward. I converted this for werewolf and it worked, and it obviously useful in dungeon crawler rpgs. The idea is that I will really only have these five scenes planned out. I will need to look at the major mechanics and try and have ready ideas of how one or two could be used for each room. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8619-aforgeforthoughts","name":"AForgeForThoughts","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-15T06:22:45-07:00","id":969848,"title":"","content":"Mage is definitely the most complex game that I'm willing to run - and it's not even that the mechanics are difficult, really (they're often just roll modifiers or dice tricks), but there's just such a huge quantity of things to remember, and characters are able to get information at the drop of a hat so there's a lot of pressure on a storyteller to have answers ready to go.<br /> <br /> To help myself as a GM, I've created an offline TiddlyWiki file on my laptop where I keep a bunch of cheat sheets and rules references (as well as campaign notes) all hyperlinked together. If I need to double-check the Paradox rules, I just type Paradox into the search bar, and the wiki page that comes up also links to the &quot;Spellcasting&quot; page and the entries for the various Paradox Conditions. I've got the PCs' character sheets stored there, with links from their Merits to descriptions of those merits and et cetera et cetera - it's a huge help.<br /> <br /> To help the players, I tend to try to construct the early sessions along the lines of video game tutorial missions. I pick a mechanic or game concept to highlight - Scrutiny, for instance, or improvised spellcasting, or the Shadow, or Sympathy - and try to emphasize it in the story by creating problems that can be solved with that mechanic...and crucially, I tell them how the mechanic can help, rather than just waiting for them to stumble across it. The point is for them to learn, not for them to accumulate some kind of invisible Roleplaying Tough Guy Cred. If I'm playing with people who already know the ins and outs of the system, then in lieu of a mechanical tutorial I focus on giving the players a guided tour of the highlights of the setting - letting them learn about the city's central mystery, the most important NPCs, and so forth. And since I started doing this a few years ago, I've found that my players have been much more willing to engage with these complicated systems and in-depth settings than they were before.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12136-jacobkosh","name":"jacobkosh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}